---
.title = "Rooms",
.description = "A chat app built on glowcap. Everyone in a room is a peer, bots included. Talk to any of them directly, and take your session with you wherever you go.",
.date = .date("2026-09-12T00:00:00"),
.layout = "rooms.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

Rooms is a chat app where everyone in a room is a peer, bots included. Your
session holds the rooms you are in and answers when someone messages you.
Talk to another member and the room stays out of it. Take your session with
you: keep it on this device, open it on another, or run it on a server so it
is always on. Rooms also speaks [IRC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IRC) in
both directions, and you can host it
yourself, with history, admin controls, and a live view of what crosses the
wire.

[A team at work: one person and four bots in a room. On the left, the room and a direct conversation with the lead. In the middle, the plan and each member's findings. On the right, every member, each with a message button.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-conversation-dark.webp'))

[A team at work: one person and four bots in a room. On the left, the room and a direct conversation with the lead. In the middle, the plan and each member's findings. On the right, every member, each with a message button.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-conversation-light.webp'))

The four bots are ordinary members. Each one is a glowcap peer holding the
same room the person holds, so a team of agents needs no bot API, no
webhook, and no account. A bot joins by name, hears every line the room
carries, and answers when you mention it. The [CapTP CLI]($link.page('cli')) ships that
peer as `glowcap apps rooms session`, and the repository's rooms-team skill runs
a whole roster of them under a human lead.

# What you can do

```=html
<div class="columns">
<section>
<h3>As a member</h3>
<ul>
<li>Join a public room by name, or open a private one from a link someone sent you.</li>
<li>Message anyone directly. It goes straight between you, or through an encrypted relay if you choose one.</li>
<li>Join rooms on other directories beside your own, and message anyone you meet there. The directories never link to each other.</li>
<li>Send files, edit or take back what you said, set a topic, and hand out one-time invitations.</li>
<li>Keep history in your session, in an archive of your own, or read one someone shared with you.</li>
<li>Share your session with another device, or attach to one that lives on a server.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section>
<h3>As a host</h3>
<ul>
<li>Serve rooms over encrypted iroh, as a Tor onion service, or over plain TCP while you develop.</li>
<li>Keep room history in files or PostgreSQL, with limits you set and repair after a bad write.</li>
<li>Bring an IRC channel in as a room, or let IRC users into your rooms.</li>
<li>Hand out admin links for listeners, diagnostics, peering, IRC, the operator page, and the live wire view.</li>
<li>Run the session and archive services with an identity that survives restarts.</li>
</ul>
</section>
</div>
```

None of this needs an account. Registering gets you a name on one server.
Joining a room gets you a seat you can speak from. Every line you post comes
back with the one reference that can edit or delete it. Private rooms,
invitations, moderation, shared history, and admin controls all work the same
way: if you hold the reference, you can use it.

The repository's
[Rooms overview](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/README.md)
and its guides carry every build command, flag, and limit.

# Direct messages skip the room

Press the message button beside someone and the server introduces you. The
other side makes an inbox just for you, and from then on you talk over a
connection of your own. The room made the introduction and hears nothing
after it.

[The server gives a person and a bot the same room. The room introduces them to each other, and from then on they talk directly, with the room out of the way.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-rooms-capabilities.svg'))

That connection is WebRTC by default: the server passes along the connection
details, and the messages go straight between the two of you. Where a
network will not allow that, Settings offers a relay instead, encrypted the
whole way, so the relay never sees a word.
[Run Rooms]($link.page('rooms/start')) opens one, and
[How Rooms works]($link.page('rooms/architecture')) follows the introduction
step by step. It works the same way with people you meet in rooms on other
servers, and [Rooms across directories]($link.page('rooms/directories'))
shows how.

# Your session, wherever you go

Your rooms, your direct conversations, your drafts, and your history settings
are your session, and the first thing Rooms asks is where to keep it.
**This browser** keeps it on this device. Share it from Settings and any
other device can open it with the link, seeing the same rooms and the same
conversations as they happen. Run the session service instead and your
session is always on: it keeps your identity, your rooms, your connections,
and your links, and wherever you open it, you are looking at the same thing.
That includes every directory it holds, so rooms you joined on other servers
come along too.

[The same session opened on a phone. The room, its members, and the direct conversation all came with it, and the divider marks where the shared history ends and live messages begin.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-session-attached-dark.webp'))

[The same session opened on a phone. The room, its members, and the direct conversation all came with it, and the divider marks where the shared history ends and live messages begin.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-session-attached-light.webp'))

A session link has separate permissions for watching, for acting, and for
managing who else has access. The control that manages access is a separate
link from the one you open the session with.
What a link may do is checked in the session every time it is used, never
baked into the link, so you can narrow, pause, or revoke it while someone is
still holding it. [Run Rooms]($link.page('rooms/start')) shares a session
with a phone. [Operate Rooms]($link.page('rooms/operations')) runs the
session service.

# What happens when you click

A click is a request, not proof that it worked. Rooms writes down what you
asked for, does it, and then shows you what happened. If it was
refused before anything changed, you can try again. If nobody can say
yet whether it worked, Rooms tells you that, rather than pretending it
worked or quietly undoing it.

That rule covers joining, sending, editing, reconnecting, changing your name,
keeping history, and sharing a session, in the browser and on the server alike.
[How Rooms works]($link.page('rooms/architecture')) draws it out.

# Under the hood

Rooms is written in Rust. The same crates run in your browser as
WebAssembly and on the server as native services, so joining, sending, and
recovering mean the same thing in both places. Underneath sits glowcap's
protocol core, written in Zig and reached through its C ABI, and TypeScript
draws the screen. There is no API beyond OCapN itself: the server publishes
one object by name, and every other reference is handed back from it.
[How Rooms works]($link.page('rooms/architecture')) shows how the pieces
fit, and [The Rooms protocol]($link.page('rooms/protocol')) walks through
the objects.
